Deployment notes

Place both scripts in /etc/slurm/ (or another location readable by the slurmctld user) and make them executable. Then add to slurm.conf:
PrologSlurmctld=/etc/slurm/prolog_gpu_tag.py
EpilogSlurmctld=/etc/slurm/epilog_gpu_tag.py
PrologFlags=Alloc
Restart slurmctld and test with a short salloc --gres=gpu:1.

Note: SLURM_JOB_GPUS supplies indices; map them to serials via your existing inventory or nvidia-smi -L if the controller can see the GPUs.

chmod +x them, point PrologSlurmctld / EpilogSlurmctld at them with PrologFlags=Alloc, restart slurmctld, and test with a short salloc --gres=gpu:1.

Note


You can only point PrologSlurmctld / EpilogSlurmctld at one script each in slurm.conf. Slurm does not chain multiple independent prolog/epilog scripts the way some other schedulers do.So you have three practical options:

Recommended: thin wrapper that runs both (keeps concerns separate)Keep your existing prolog as-is and make a small wrapper the official PrologSlurmctld:


#!/bin/bash
# /etc/slurm/prolog_slurmctld_wrapper.sh
set -euo pipefail

# Run existing logic first (or second — order is up to you)
/etc/slurm/your_existing_prolog.sh "$@"

# Then the GPU tagger (non-blocking; it already exits 0 on failure)
/etc/slurm/prolog_gpu_tag.py "$@"

Same pattern for epilog.PrologSlurmctld=/etc/slurm/prolog_slurmctld_wrapper.sh
EpilogSlurmctld=/etc/slurm/epilog_slurmctld_wrapper.sh
PrologFlags=Alloc

